WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.000
What’s the best way
to stay healthy

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.250
in the face of so much
conflicting nutrition information?

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.559
Well, ideally, you would
go to the source.

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:09.559
The gold standard;

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:11.150
the peer-reviewed
medical literature.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.330
Read through stacks of
the latest medical journals.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.430
But who’s got
time for that?

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:17.550
I do!

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.760
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.430
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:27.980
Did you know that plant-based diets
are gaining in popularity among athletes?

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.140
In our first story, we discover why
folks are drawn to plant-based diets

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.100
for athletic performance and recovery.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.020
Consumer interest in eating
plant-based has surged

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.110
over the last few years, and
athletes are no exception.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.460
While in the past, meat was
seen as an irreplaceable

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.340
performance-enhancing food,
today the trend is developing

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.320
in the opposite direction,
thanks in part to documentaries

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.840
like The Game Changers,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.960
for which I was honored to play
a role as scientific advisor.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.510
Several high-profile athletes
from heavyweight champion boxers

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.180
to tennis players have
tried fueling with plants.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.610
Athletes have increasingly
been adopting plant-based diets

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.440
not only for the
related health beneﬁts

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.100
but for perceived improvements
in endurance performance.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.700
In fact, even by 2016, there
were reports of up to a third

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.479
of ultra-endurance runners,
for example, shunning meat.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:22.370
Increasing plant-based foods
may boost vasodilatory—

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:26.680
meaning artery-dilating, antioxidant,
and anti-inflammatory properties

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.210
of the diet, which can
lead to improved blood flow,

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:33.240
reduced oxidative stress
and inflammation, and thus,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.390
theoretically enhance
endurance performance,

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:38.450
reduce muscle damage,
and speed recovery.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:41.100
Exercise itself can release free
radicals that can also lead

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.530
to muscle fatigue, reduced athletic
performance, and impaired recovery,

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.780
but the antioxidants
concentrated in plant foods

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:48.860
can help extinguish them.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:49.860
Here’s the level of oxidative
stress before and after running

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:50.860
for a few hours, a doubling of
this oxidative stress marker,

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:51.860
but that’s without blueberries.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:52.860
Eat a cup and a half of
blueberries an hour before the run

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:53.860
and you can significantly
blunt the stress.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:54.860
No wonder that eating
even a cup of blueberries

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:55.860
may improve exercise performance,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:56.860
whereas eating meat could
actually make things worse.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:57.860
Same with inflammation.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:58.860
Shifting to a dietary
pattern with more plants

00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.860
and less animal-sourced food has
been shown to attenuate inﬂammation.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:00.860
A systematic review and meta-analysis
comparing common biomarkers

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:02.299
of inflammation found
that meat- free diets

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:04.869
appeared to be
favorable in all cases.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:07.600
And not just inflammation,
but immune status.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.830
Having a strong immune system
is important for athletes,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.580
especially endurance athletes,

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.530
as they are often
immunocompromised,

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.950
which increases risk of upper
respiratory tract infection.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.959
After a marathon, there can
be about 6-fold higher odds

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:22.670
of coming down with an infection

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.620
and after an ultramarathon as
many as 68 percent fall ill

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.330
within the ensuing two weeks.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:31.760
But hey, a better immune system
could translate into less illness,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.959
which means more time training
for the plant-based athlete,

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:38.400
though this has yet
to be studied directly.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.610
We also know there’s an ergogenic,
meaning performance-enhancing,

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:45.860
effect to nitrates, and nitrates
in the bloodstream of vegetarians

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.610
is about 20 percent higher
and in vegans 40 percent higher

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:52.209
likely due to their eating
more nitrate-rich vegetables

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:54.190
such as beets, spinach,
and other greens.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.250
And then there are all
the health benefits

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.400
that could boost
performance in the long term.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.220
It is well-documented that plant-
based diets reduce the risk

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:04.700
of chronic disease such as
cardiovascular disease, diabetes,

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.230
metabolic syndrome, and
cancer and all-cause mortality,

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:11.530
meaning living a
significantly longer life.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:15.840
But do high-performing athletes
really need cardiovascular protection?

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.140
Surprisingly, (and that’s
why I did this video),

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.120
endurance athletes may have
more advanced atherosclerosis,

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:26.239
more heart muscle damage,
compared to sedentary individuals.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:29.730
Male athletes had a higher
percentage of atherosclerotic plaques

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.920
in their coronary arteries
compared with sedentary males.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.580
A higher prevalence of
high coronary artery calcium

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.970
a greater number of
atherosclerotic plaques

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.040
including multi-vessel plaques,
and a greater proportion

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.940
squeezing off blood
flow more than 50 percent.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.200
Marathon runners, found
to have increased total

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.410
atherosclerotic plaque volume—

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.299
calcified plaques,
non-calcified plaques.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:58.379
Paradoxically, worse atherosclerosis
in their coronary arteries,

00:03:58.379 --> 00:04:01.459
which may then translate into more
damage to the heart muscle itself—

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:04.540
three times more than
matched sedentary individuals.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.849
They are running, the risk
of coronary events.

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:11.190
It’s the kind of heart damage
you see after heart attacks.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:12.340
But wait, why?

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:17.100
Why do studies show that well-trained
athletes are at signiﬁcant risk

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.509
for atherosclerosis
and heart damage?

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:21.340
It may not be because
they’re overstressing

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.870
their heart with movement

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:28.169
but rather overstressing their heart
with saturated fat and cholesterol.

00:04:28.169 --> 00:04:32.280
Endurance athletes can eat
5, 6, 7,000 calories a day.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.110
So, if you’re eating
twice the Big Macs,

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.630
no wonder their poor hearts
are getting hammered.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:41.280
That’s where plant-based
diets come in,

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.919
the only diet shown to be
able to reverse the progression

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:46.430
of heart disease in the
majority of patients.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:50.000
Yeah, such diets may also
contribute to improved performance

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.410
and accelerated recovery,
but most importantly,

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:58.090
will allow you to recover and
maintain your long-term health.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:02.155
Athletic performance
suffers, when you’re dead.

00:05:02.671 --> 00:05:07.169
In our next story, we look at the benefits
of reducing acid-forming foods

00:05:07.169 --> 00:05:10.210
and boosting alkaline-forming foods.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:14.169
Theoretically, bathing your muscles in
an alkaline environment should enable

00:05:14.169 --> 00:05:18.440
faster acid removal from muscle
cells, delaying the muscle fatigue

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.460
that's due to the buildup
of lactic acid in the muscle.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:24.930
Given these buffering effects,
no wonder sodium bicarbonate —

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.560
in other words, baking soda —
has been found to have such

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.780
significant ergogenic
or performance-enhancing

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.699
effects on muscular endurance.

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:36.680
The problem with loading with
baking soda is that it frequently

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:41.300
causes severe gastrointestinal
distress, and at standard doses

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.440
you could easily take in twice the
recommended upper daily limit

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:51.470
of sodium in just that one load.
Therefore, what about a low-acid diet,

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:54.010
which focuses on high intakes
of fruits and vegetables?

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:57.660
That may be an attractive
alternative to bicarbonate loading

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.970
for improving anaerobic
exercise performance,

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:04.260
meaning short burst
activity like sprinting.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:07.350
Today's diets are acid-forming,
meaning higher in animal foods

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.800
with fewer vegetables and fruits,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:12.509
whereas in general, the alkaline-
promoting diet is centered around

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:17.170
whole plant foods, with few processed
foods and less meat, dairy and eggs,

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:21.870
which are accepted as acid-forming
foods. Although alkalizing chemicals

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:26.849
such as sodium bicarbonate have
been shown to consistently improve

00:06:26.849 --> 00:06:30.789
performance, alkalizing diets do
not demonstrate the same effect.

00:06:30.789 --> 00:06:32.889
A review of ten studies
that investigated

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:36.990
the effect of high versus low dietary
acid loads on athletic performance

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:40.639
did not find consistently improved
exercise performance

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:44.099
at maximal or submaximal
exercise intensities.

00:06:44.099 --> 00:06:48.000
However, maybe they just
didn't go alkaline enough?

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.669
For example, in this study

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:51.590
they had people eat more
fruits and vegetables

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.539
and less meat, cheese,
cereals, and eggs,

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:58.690
but saw no difference in any
performance-related parameters.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:03.240
But a sufficiently alkaline diet
is characterized by the production

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:07.990
of alkaline urine, a pH
of pee at least 7 or higher.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:10.520
And if you look at what
the participants achieved,

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:12.410
most failed to meet
the benchmark,

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:16.720
so their diet may just not have
been alkaline-forming enough.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:20.010
In this study showing enhanced
400-meter sprint performance,

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:22.830
they were able to swap out
enough meat, eggs, cheese

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:26.539
and cereal products, and swap
in enough fruits and vegetables

00:07:26.539 --> 00:07:29.099
that they were just barely
able to make 7,

00:07:29.099 --> 00:07:32.340
and they did get a little performance
boost, suggesting that it's possible

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:36.370
to improve sprint performance by
consuming alkalizing natural foods

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:39.990
and beverages, without
the ingestion of baking soda.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:44.030
Thus, an alkalizing diet may be
an easy and natural way

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:47.140
to enhance performance.
However, the performance

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:50.909
enhancement was only about 2% —
just a few seconds.

00:07:50.909 --> 00:07:53.120
But check out this study.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:55.940
The same general strategy:
more fruits and vegetables,

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:58.900
and less the acid-promoting
foods such as meats, cheeses,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:02.750
and refined grains. But they
weren't messing around.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.520
Six to eight cups of vegetables,

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.440
plus more than four
servings of fruit a day, got

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:14.310
their urine up over 7, and a 21%
performance enhancement.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.020
That's extraordinary.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:19.460
Now this doesn't necessarily mean
it has anything to do with the pH.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:23.789
I mean they ate more vegetables,
which contain dietary nitrates,

00:08:23.789 --> 00:08:27.919
which alone acutely improve
exercise performance.

00:08:27.919 --> 00:08:30.310
It's possible that nitrates
might have enhanced

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:33.090
exercise performance
during the low-acid trial.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:35.620
However, that would entail
extracting more energy

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:38.159
from every breath,
and they didn't find that.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:41.419
But any time you make huge
changes in peoples' diets,

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:45.080
it's hard to pinpoint exactly what
made the critical difference.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:48.320
But these extra benefits
are a feature, not a bug.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.589
Because a more alkaline-forming
diet is rich in fruits and vegetables,

00:08:51.589 --> 00:08:55.510
and deplete of many unhealthy foods,
a low-acid diet could not only provide

00:08:55.510 --> 00:09:01.440
exercise performance benefits, but
might also reduce chronic disease risk.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.019
Any performance benefits
in non-athletes?

00:09:05.019 --> 00:09:08.750
The effect of an alkaline diet
on body composition

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:12.410
and aerobic exercise performance
of sedentary women.

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:15.779
A randomized controlled trial,
swapping in vegetable protein

00:09:15.779 --> 00:09:20.120
sources like legumes for
animal protein sources, and...

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:25.230
a greater decrease in weight, body
fat mass, lactic acid accumulation,

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:28.420
and perceived exertion levels,
all the while exhibiting a boost

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.260
in exercise performance
duration and VO2 max levels,

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:33.890
which is a measure of fitness.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:37.750
Again pH could just be one of many
explanations why eating healthier

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:40.380
could make you perform
better, but either way

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:44.010
we should recommend athletes
of all ages focus on consuming

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:49.139
ample amounts of fruits and vegetables,
if only to maintain long-term health.

00:09:49.655 --> 00:09:53.520
Finally today, we go
back in time, way back,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:57.450
to compare the diets of the
Roman gladiator “barley men”

00:09:57.450 --> 00:10:00.000
to the modern Spartans of today.

00:10:00.486 --> 00:10:03.920
Recently, the remains of
dozens of Roman gladiators

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.300
were discovered in a mass grave.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:09.350
The clue to their identities
were the rather distinct types

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:11.880
of mortal injuries they found,

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.960
like being speared in
the head with a trident.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:18.160
Using just their skeletons
they were able to reconstruct

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:22.329
the death blows, show just
how buff they really were,

00:10:22.329 --> 00:10:27.600
and even try to reconstruct
their diet of barley and beans.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.279
You can look at carbon isotopes and
see what kinds of plants they ate;

00:10:31.279 --> 00:10:34.550
nitrogen isotopes reflect any
intake of animal protein.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:36.380
You can also look at the
Sulphur in their bones

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:39.649
and the amount of strontium,
leading commentators to submit

00:10:39.649 --> 00:10:43.480
that the best athletes
in ancient Rome ate

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:46.000
largely plant-based diets.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.709
Then there were the legionnaires,
the Roman army troopers,

00:10:49.709 --> 00:10:53.270
famed for their abilities, also
eating a similar kind of diet,

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:56.149
suggesting “the best fighters
in the ancient world

00:10:56.149 --> 00:10:57.899
were essentially vegetarian.”

00:10:57.899 --> 00:11:01.780
So, if the so-called
perfect fighting machines,

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:06.200
the great sports heroes of the day,
were eating mostly grains and beans,

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:09.329
should that tell us anything
about sports nutrition

00:11:09.329 --> 00:11:12.570
and the preferred diets
of elite athletes?

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:16.430
Well, most of the Greeks and
Romans were basically vegetarian,

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:19.529
centering their diets around
grains, fruit, vegetables and beans,

00:11:19.529 --> 00:11:23.680
so maybe the gladiators’ diets
weren’t that remarkable.

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:27.650
Plato, for example, pushed
plants, preferring plant foods

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:29.690
for their health and efficiency.

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:35.380
So yes, the Roman gladiators
were known as the ‘‘barley men,’’

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:38.139
but is that because barley
gives you strength and stamina,

00:11:38.139 --> 00:11:41.459
or was that just the basic food
that people ate at the time,

00:11:41.459 --> 00:11:46.170
not necessarily for performance,
but because it was just so cheap?

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:51.380
Well, if you look at the modern
Spartans, the Tarahumara Indians,

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.550
the ones that run races where
they kick a ball for oh,

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:58.480
75 miles just for the fun of it,
running all day, all night,

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:02.470
and all day, maybe 150 miles
if they’re feeling in the mood.

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:05.120
What do you get if you win?

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:09.780
A special popularity with the ladies
(although how much of a reward

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:12.190
that would actually prove to be
for a man who had been running

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:14.110
for two days straight is questionable;

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:18.050
though, maybe their endurance
extends to other dimensions).

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:21.600
“Probably not since the
days of the ancient Spartans

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:26.250
has a people achieved such a high
state of extreme physical conditioning.”

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:28.570
And what did they eat?

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:31.089
The same kind of 75 to
80 percent starch diet

00:12:31.089 --> 00:12:33.950
based on beans, corn, and squash.

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:38.029
And, they had the cholesterol
levels to prove it,

00:12:38.029 --> 00:12:41.640
total cholesterol levels down at an
essentially heart attack proof 136.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:46.029
And it’s not just some
special genetics they have—

00:12:46.029 --> 00:12:52.010
you feed them enough egg yolks and
their cholesterol creeps right up.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:55.639
Modern day Olympian runners
eat the same stuff.

00:12:55.639 --> 00:12:57.320
What are they eating over there in Kenya?

00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:01.420
A 99 percent vegetarian diet centered
mostly around various starches.

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:06.149
But as in all these cases, is
their remarkable physical prowess

00:13:06.149 --> 00:13:09.790
because of their diets, or
in spite of their diets?

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:11.411
Or have nothing to do with their diets?

00:13:11.411 --> 00:13:15.310
You don’t know…until
you put it to the test.

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:19.730
In spite of well-documented health
benefits of more plant-based diets,

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:22.899
less is known regarding the effects
of these diets on athletic performance.

00:13:22.899 --> 00:13:27.889
So, they compared elite vegetarian
and omnivore endurance athletes

00:13:27.889 --> 00:13:29.890
for aerobic fitness and strength.

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:32.550
So, comparing oxygen
utilization on the treadmill,

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.700
and quad strength with leg extensions.

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:38.350
And the vegetarians beat out
their omnivore counterparts

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:42.550
for cardiorespiratory fitness,
but their strength didn’t differ.

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:45.080
Suggesting, in the very least,
that vegetarian diets

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.829
don’t compromise athletic performance.

00:13:47.829 --> 00:13:50.389
But this was a cross-sectional study.

00:13:50.389 --> 00:13:54.769
Maybe the veg athletes were just
fitter because they trained harder?

00:13:54.769 --> 00:13:57.120
Like in the National Runners' Health Study

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:00.910
looking at thousands of runners:
vegetarian runners were recorded

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:03.900
running significantly
more on a weekly basis;

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:07.070
so, maybe that explains
their superior fitness.

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:11.440
Though, maybe their superior fitness
explains their greater distances.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:14.570
Other cross-sectional studies
have found no differences

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:18.660
in physical fitness between
vegetarian and non-vegetarian athletes,

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:23.190
or even worse performance, as in this
study of vegetarian athletes in India.

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:26.769
Of course, there could be socioeconomic
or other confounding factors.

00:14:26.769 --> 00:14:30.740
That’s why we need interventional
studies to put different diets

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:34.250
to the test and then compare
physical performance,

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:37.160
which we’ll explore next.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:42.621
In 1896, the aptly named
James Parsley evidently led

00:14:42.621 --> 00:14:46.389
a successful vegetarian
cycling club to victory,

00:14:46.389 --> 00:14:50.970
their competitors evidently having
to "eat crow with their beef."

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:53.709
Evidently some Belgian
put it to the test in 1904,

00:14:53.709 --> 00:14:57.100
with those eating more plant-based
supposedly lifting some weight

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:58.769
like 80 percent more times,

00:14:58.769 --> 00:15:02.459
but I couldn't find the
primary source in English.

00:15:02.459 --> 00:15:07.139
This I could find though: a famous
series of experiments at Yale,

00:15:07.139 --> 00:15:13.509
published more than a century ago, on the
influence of flesh-eating on endurance.

00:15:13.509 --> 00:15:18.199
Forty-nine people were compared:
regular athletes (mostly Yale students),

00:15:18.199 --> 00:15:21.040
vegetarian athletes, and then
just sedentary vegetarians.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:27.279
"The experiment furnished a severe test
of the claims of those flesh-abstainers."

00:15:27.279 --> 00:15:32.550
Much to the researchers' surprise,
the results seemed to vindicate

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:37.649
the vegetarians, suggesting that
not eating meat leads to far greater

00:15:37.649 --> 00:15:42.779
endurance compared to those accustomed
to the ordinary American diet.

00:15:42.779 --> 00:15:47.100
Check it out: the first endurance test was
how many minutes straight you could

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:53.949
hold out your arms horizontally:
flesh-eaters versus flesh abstainers.

00:15:53.949 --> 00:15:57.120
The regular Yale athletes were
able to keep their hands out

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:59.240
for about 10 minutes on average.

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:02.760
It's harder than it sounds;
give it a try...

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:08.480
OK, but those eating vegetarian
did like five times better.

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:16.580
The meat-eater maximum was only
half that of the vegetarian average.

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:19.509
Only two meat eaters
even hit 15 minutes,

00:16:19.509 --> 00:16:22.540
whereas more than two-thirds
of the meat-avoiders did.

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:25.170
None of the regular diet
folks hit a half hour,

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.970
whereas nearly half of
the healthier eaters did,

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:31.620
including nine that exceeded an
hour, four that exceeded two hours,

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:36.810
and one guy going for
more than three hours.

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.790
How many deep knee
bends can you do?

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:43.769
One athlete could do more
than 1,000—averaging 383—

00:16:43.769 --> 00:16:48.259
but they got creamed even
by the sedentary plant-eaters.

00:16:48.259 --> 00:16:53.660
That's the crazy thing; even
the sedentary abstainers

00:16:53.660 --> 00:16:56.889
surpassed the exercising flesh-eaters.

00:16:56.889 --> 00:16:58.410
The sedentary abstainers were,

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:01.550
in most cases, physicians
who sat on their butts all day.

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:05.910
I want a doctor that that can do
a thousand deep knee bends!

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:10.530
And then in terms of recovery, all those
deep knee bends left everyone sore,

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:13.069
but more so among those eating meat.

00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:16.840
Among the vegetarians, of two that
did like 2,000 knee bends, one went

00:17:16.840 --> 00:17:20.880
straight off to the track to run and
another went on to their nursing duties.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:25.319
On the other hand, among the
meat-eaters one guy reached 254,

00:17:25.319 --> 00:17:29.390
went down once more and couldn't
get back up, had to be carried away,

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:35.350
and was incapacitated for days; another
impaired for weeks after fainting.

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:39.630
It may be inferred without reasonable
doubt, concluded the once skeptical

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:44.840
Yale researcher, that the meat-eating
group of athletes was very far inferior

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:49.520
in endurance to the vegetarians,
even the sedentary ones.

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:53.430
What could account for
this remarkable difference?

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:57.690
Some claimed that flesh foods contained
some kind of "fatigue poisons,"

00:17:57.690 --> 00:18:01.650
but one German researcher who detailed
his own experiments with athletes

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:05.630
offered a more prosaic answer.
In his book on what looks like

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:09.780
physiological studies of
uber-driving vegetarians—

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:12.250
I told you I only know English—

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:16.260
he conjectured that the apparent
vegetarian superiority was just due

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:19.309
to their tremendous determination
to prove their point

00:18:19.309 --> 00:18:23.650
and spread their propaganda,
so they just make a greater effort

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:27.500
in any contest than do
their meat-eating rivals.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:30.640
The Yale researchers were worried
about this, and so special pains

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:34.630
were taken to stimulate the flesh-eaters
to the utmost, appealing to

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:41.620
their college pride. Don't let those lousy
vegetarians beat the "Yale spirit."

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:44.840
The experiments made it
into The New York Times.

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:49.240
Yale's flesh-eating athletes—
sounds like a zombie movie—

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:54.470
beaten in severe endurance tests.
Yale professor believes that

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:58.640
he has shown definitely the inferiority
in strength and endurance tests

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:01.520
of meat eaters compared to
those who do not eat meat.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:05.750
Some of Yale's most successful
athletes took part in the strength tests,

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:10.220
and Professor Fisher declares they
were obliged to admit their inferiority.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:16.559
How has the truth of this result
been so long obscured?

00:19:16.559 --> 00:19:20.840
One reason, Professor Fisher
suggested, is that vegetarians

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:25.309
are their own worst enemy.
In their fanaticism, they jump

00:19:25.309 --> 00:19:28.980
from the premise that meat eating
is wrong—often based on scripture

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:34.530
or some kind of dogma—and jump
from that to meat-eating is unhealthy.

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:39.120
That's not how science works
and such logical leaps get them

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:44.799
dismissed as zealots and prevent
any genuine scientific investigation.

00:19:44.799 --> 00:19:48.190
Lots of science, even back then,
was pointing a distinct trend

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:52.210
towards more plant-based eating,
and yet the word vegetarian—

00:19:52.210 --> 00:19:57.600
even 110 years ago—had
such a bad, preachy rap

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:02.039
that many were loath to concede
the science in its favor.

00:20:02.039 --> 00:20:06.289
The proper scientific attitude is to study
the question of meat-eating in precisely

00:20:06.289 --> 00:20:12.000
the same manner as one would
study the question of anything else.

